1
00:00:00,800 --> 00:00:04,633
In 2025, the President of the
United States started calling

2
00:00:04,700 --> 00:00:06,733
Canada something strange.

3
00:00:06,800 --> 00:00:08,600
The fifty-first state.

4
00:00:08,667 --> 00:00:14,767
Canada's leaders answered fast: Canada
is not for sale, and it never will be.

5
00:00:15,133 --> 00:00:18,467
It sounds like a joke between
two friendly neighbors.

6
00:00:18,533 --> 00:00:23,833
They share the longest border on Earth,
about five thousand five hundred miles,

7
00:00:23,900 --> 00:00:26,833
and almost no soldiers guard it.

8
00:00:27,233 --> 00:00:33,167
Canada is the second largest country on
Earth, bigger than the entire United States.

9
00:00:33,233 --> 00:00:37,967
But it has only about forty million
people, fewer than California.

10
00:00:38,033 --> 00:00:42,633
And most of them live within a
hundred miles of the American border.

11
00:00:43,033 --> 00:00:46,833
But here is what most Americans
never learned in school.

12
00:00:46,900 --> 00:00:50,033
The United States has tried
to take Canada before.

13
00:00:50,100 --> 00:00:51,333
More than once.

14
00:00:51,400 --> 00:00:52,933
With real armies.

15
00:00:53,000 --> 00:00:56,367
And every single time, it went badly.

16
00:00:57,400 --> 00:01:04,567
In 1775, the American Revolution had just
begun, and the rebels had a bold idea.

17
00:01:04,633 --> 00:01:07,167
Canada should be the fourteenth colony.

18
00:01:07,233 --> 00:01:11,400
So two American armies
marched north to take Quebec.

19
00:01:11,867 --> 00:01:14,333
One army sailed up Lake Champlain.

20
00:01:14,400 --> 00:01:19,233
The other, led by Benedict Arnold, dragged
boats through the forests of Maine.

21
00:01:19,300 --> 00:01:24,400
On the last night of 1775, they
attacked Quebec City in a blizzard.

22
00:01:24,467 --> 00:01:27,233
The general leading the attack was killed.

23
00:01:27,300 --> 00:01:29,133
Arnold was shot in the leg.

24
00:01:29,200 --> 00:01:31,533
The invasion collapsed.

25
00:01:31,967 --> 00:01:34,900
Smallpox tore through the American camps.

26
00:01:34,967 --> 00:01:41,067
In the spring of 1776, British reinforcements
sailed up the Saint Lawrence River,

27
00:01:41,133 --> 00:01:45,133
and what was left of the
American army retreated south.

28
00:01:45,533 --> 00:01:47,133
And Benedict Arnold?

29
00:01:47,200 --> 00:01:50,967
A few years later, he switched
sides and joined the British.

30
00:01:51,033 --> 00:01:54,500
In America, his name still means traitor.

31
00:01:54,933 --> 00:01:58,033
Even then, America did
not give up the dream.

32
00:01:58,100 --> 00:02:03,167
Its first constitution, the Articles
of Confederation, had a special rule:

33
00:02:03,233 --> 00:02:06,367
Canada could join the
union whenever it wanted.

34
00:02:06,433 --> 00:02:08,767
No other colony got that offer.

35
00:02:08,833 --> 00:02:11,300
Canada never said yes.

36
00:02:11,700 --> 00:02:16,467
And when the Revolution ended, tens of
thousands of Americans who had stayed loyal

37
00:02:16,533 --> 00:02:18,733
to the king fled north.

38
00:02:18,800 --> 00:02:22,300
Some of the people who built
English Canada were Americans who

39
00:02:22,367 --> 00:02:25,100
refused to become American.

40
00:02:26,133 --> 00:02:28,300
Then came 1812.

41
00:02:28,367 --> 00:02:32,900
The United States declared war on
Britain, and the plan was simple:

42
00:02:32,967 --> 00:02:38,067
invade Canada while the British were
busy fighting Napoleon in Europe.

43
00:02:38,433 --> 00:02:41,233
Thomas Jefferson predicted
it would be easy.

44
00:02:41,300 --> 00:02:45,667
Taking Canada, he wrote, would
be a mere matter of marching.

45
00:02:46,067 --> 00:02:47,167
It was not.

46
00:02:47,233 --> 00:02:52,233
Two months later, an American general
surrendered the entire city of Detroit without

47
00:02:52,300 --> 00:02:53,267
a fight.

48
00:02:53,333 --> 00:02:56,933
Attack after attack
along the border failed.

49
00:02:57,300 --> 00:03:00,333
The British also had powerful allies.

50
00:03:00,400 --> 00:03:04,667
The Shawnee leader Tecumseh and his
confederacy fought on their side,

51
00:03:04,733 --> 00:03:10,100
hoping a British victory would stop
American settlers from taking their land.

52
00:03:10,533 --> 00:03:16,433
In 1813, American troops burned the
town of York, today called Toronto.

53
00:03:16,500 --> 00:03:19,567
A year later, the
British came for revenge.

54
00:03:19,633 --> 00:03:24,167
They marched into Washington and set
fire to the Capitol and the White House.

55
00:03:24,233 --> 00:03:27,567
The president had to flee his own capital.

56
00:03:28,000 --> 00:03:30,733
Weeks later, the British
attacked Baltimore.

57
00:03:30,800 --> 00:03:31,967
They failed.

58
00:03:32,033 --> 00:03:35,500
An American lawyer who watched
the bombardment wrote a poem about

59
00:03:35,567 --> 00:03:38,167
the flag still flying over the fort.

60
00:03:38,233 --> 00:03:42,767
It became the national anthem:
The Star-Spangled Banner.

61
00:03:43,167 --> 00:03:47,000
The war ended in a treaty
that changed almost nothing.

62
00:03:47,067 --> 00:03:52,433
After two and a half years of fighting,
the border stayed exactly where it was.

63
00:03:53,433 --> 00:03:56,333
The next fight was over
the Pacific Northwest.

64
00:03:56,400 --> 00:04:03,033
In 1844, American expansionists wanted
everything up to the edge of Russian Alaska.

65
00:04:03,100 --> 00:04:07,033
Their slogan: fifty-four forty or fight.

66
00:04:07,433 --> 00:04:11,367
But President Polk was already
heading for war with Mexico.

67
00:04:11,433 --> 00:04:16,467
So in 1846 he signed a compromise
with Britain, and the border was

68
00:04:16,533 --> 00:04:21,833
drawn along the forty-ninth
parallel, where it still runs today.

69
00:04:22,200 --> 00:04:26,033
The fear of America also
helped create modern Canada.

70
00:04:26,100 --> 00:04:32,367
In 1867, the British colonies united into
one country, partly to stand together

71
00:04:32,433 --> 00:04:34,400
against their giant neighbor.

72
00:04:34,467 --> 00:04:39,400
That same year, the United States
bought Alaska, and Canada found itself

73
00:04:39,467 --> 00:04:43,433
squeezed between American
land on both sides.

74
00:04:43,833 --> 00:04:45,600
They had reason to worry.

75
00:04:45,667 --> 00:04:50,933
After the Civil War, thousands of Irish
American veterans, called the Fenians,

76
00:04:51,000 --> 00:04:55,867
launched raids into Canada, hoping
to trade it for Irish independence.

77
00:04:55,933 --> 00:04:59,433
They were beaten back,
but the message was clear.

78
00:05:00,433 --> 00:05:03,733
By the twentieth century, the
two countries were friends.

79
00:05:03,800 --> 00:05:10,167
But in 1930, the US Army approved a secret
plan for a war with the British Empire.

80
00:05:10,233 --> 00:05:16,600
It was called War Plan Red, and in
it, Canada had a code name: Crimson.

81
00:05:16,967 --> 00:05:19,100
The plan was to strike first.

82
00:05:19,167 --> 00:05:24,233
Seize the port of Halifax, cut the
railways, take Montreal and Quebec,

83
00:05:24,300 --> 00:05:28,667
and grab the Great Lakes region
before Britain could send help.

84
00:05:29,067 --> 00:05:31,200
And Canada had a plan too.

85
00:05:31,267 --> 00:05:37,200
In 1921, a Canadian officer named James
Sutherland Brown wrote Defence Scheme

86
00:05:37,267 --> 00:05:43,433
Number One: a surprise attack on American
cities like Seattle, Detroit and Albany,

87
00:05:43,500 --> 00:05:46,267
to slow the Americans down.

88
00:05:46,700 --> 00:05:49,000
Neither plan was ever used.

89
00:05:49,067 --> 00:05:54,633
War Plan Red was withdrawn in 1939,
when the real enemy turned out to be

90
00:05:54,700 --> 00:05:59,800
Nazi Germany, and it
stayed secret until 1974.

91
00:06:00,167 --> 00:06:06,167
Before that, an update in 1935 even
approved the use of poison gas,

92
00:06:06,233 --> 00:06:12,533
and Congress paid for new airfields near
the border, disguised as civilian airports.

93
00:06:13,500 --> 00:06:17,600
Today, Canada and the United States
trade more than nine hundred billion

94
00:06:17,667 --> 00:06:19,100
dollars a year.

95
00:06:19,167 --> 00:06:22,800
Cars, food, electricity, metals.

96
00:06:22,867 --> 00:06:27,567
Many products cross the border several
times before they are finished.

97
00:06:27,967 --> 00:06:32,633
The Ambassador Bridge, between Detroit
and Windsor, carries about a quarter

98
00:06:32,700 --> 00:06:35,567
of all the trade between
the two countries.

99
00:06:35,633 --> 00:06:39,867
And dams in Quebec send electricity
south to power homes in New

100
00:06:39,933 --> 00:06:42,067
York and New England.

101
00:06:42,500 --> 00:06:43,900
And there is oil.

102
00:06:43,967 --> 00:06:48,200
Canada sends the United States more
crude oil than any other country,

103
00:06:48,267 --> 00:06:52,800
around sixty percent of all
the crude America imports.

104
00:06:53,200 --> 00:06:54,833
Then there is the Arctic.

105
00:06:54,900 --> 00:07:00,200
As the ice melts, a new shipping route is
opening through Canada's northern islands.

106
00:07:00,267 --> 00:07:03,067
Canada says those waters are Canadian.

107
00:07:03,133 --> 00:07:05,667
Washington says they are international.

108
00:07:05,733 --> 00:07:10,733
Put Alaska, Canada and Greenland on one
map, and you can see why the top of the

109
00:07:10,800 --> 00:07:13,233
world matters so much.

110
00:07:13,667 --> 00:07:18,167
In 2025, the fifty-first
state talk came with tariffs.

111
00:07:18,233 --> 00:07:22,800
Canadians booed the American anthem at
hockey games, and many stopped buying

112
00:07:22,867 --> 00:07:24,467
American products.

113
00:07:24,533 --> 00:07:29,267
A friendship that survived two
world wars suddenly felt very cold.

114
00:07:29,333 --> 00:07:33,500
When Canada's new prime minister visited
the White House, he said it to the

115
00:07:33,567 --> 00:07:38,067
president's face: some
places are never for sale.

116
00:07:39,100 --> 00:07:40,433
Look at the map again.

117
00:07:40,500 --> 00:07:41,233
Greenland.

118
00:07:41,300 --> 00:07:42,567
The Panama Canal.

119
00:07:42,633 --> 00:07:43,500
Canada.

120
00:07:43,567 --> 00:07:47,367
The same idea keeps coming
back: more land, more resources,

121
00:07:47,433 --> 00:07:49,667
more control of the routes.

122
00:07:50,067 --> 00:07:52,600
But Canada is the clearest lesson of all.

123
00:07:52,667 --> 00:07:55,167
America tried force, and it failed.

124
00:07:55,233 --> 00:08:00,167
What finally worked was the opposite:
trade, trust, and an open border.

125
00:08:00,233 --> 00:08:05,367
Since 1814, the two countries have
not fired a single shot at each other.

126
00:08:05,433 --> 00:08:11,433
Their soldiers fought side by side in two
world wars, in Korea and in Afghanistan.

127
00:08:11,500 --> 00:08:15,200
Whether that lesson still
holds is the real question.

128
00:08:16,200 --> 00:08:21,300
If you want to see the other places on
America's list, watch our videos on Greenland

129
00:08:21,367 --> 00:08:23,100
and the Panama Canal.

130
00:08:23,167 --> 00:08:27,667
And subscribe, so you don't
miss what's behind the next map.
